You mean that in `Interlocked.Exchange(ref _userCanceled, 0) == 0)` the value of `_userCanceled` must be "reset" to `0`?

What can happen?
Let's assume `_userCanceled = 0`, then 0 is set, 0 returned and the comparison succeedes --> loop repeated
If `_userCanceled = 1`, then 0 is set, 1 returned and the comparison fails --> loop exited

If it's just to read the value thread-safe, then `Volatile.Read(ref _userCanceled) == 0` should be enough, as visibility is guaranteed (together with the Volatile.Write) and as `int` it's atomic on all platforms (supported).

So when I don't miss something then
```c#
        public override void CancelPendingRead()
        {
            Volatile.Write(ref _userCanceled, 1);
            _context.Input.CancelPendingRead();
        }

        ...

        public override ...
        {
            do
            {
                // ...
            } while (readResult.IsCanceled && Volatile.Read(ref _userCanceled) == 0);
        }
```
should be safe?

My point is perf here. Interlocked produces a full memory barrier, whilst Volatile.Read/Write produce half-fences, thus being cheaper.

But TBH I'm not to 100% sure if it's safe in all circumstances. I hope you have more knowledge here than I have.
